[Event "Basic tactics: the fork, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/sdDSE6ge"]
[FEN "8/5q1p/8/5NK1/6P1/3k4/8/8 b - - 11 52"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5q1p/8/5NK1/6P1/3k4/8/8_b_-_-_11_52"]

{ We will now learn the fork. One move, two targets, and neither of them can run. }
52... Ke4 { His king steps out. Now your knight can hit the king and the queen at once. [%csl Re4,Rf7][%cal Yf5d6] } 53. Nd6+ { Check! He must answer the check, so his queen has no time to escape. [%cal Rd6e4,Rd6f7] } (53. Nh6 { You attack the queen, but with no check she simply steps away. } 53... Qe7+ 54. Kh5 { She left with check, and now White is the one in trouble. }) (53. Ne7 { The knight lands next to the queen, and she just eats it. } 53... Qxe7+ 54. Kh6 { A whole knight gone for nothing. }) 53... Kd5 { The king runs, and the queen is still standing on f7. [%cal Yd6f7] } 54. Nxf7 { The queen is yours. One knight, two targets: that is a fork. } (54. Nf5 { The knight runs home and lets the queen off the hook. } 54... Ke5 55. Nh4 Qf4 { The fork is gone and White is losing. }) 1-0


[Event "Basic tactics: fork practice one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/uL3WWVrr"]
[FEN "8/8/5kp1/5n1p/4K3/8/8/6R1 w - - 2 58"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/5kp1/5n1p/4K3/8/8/6R1_w_-_-_2_58"]

{ Your knight is hunting. Look for a square that attacks two things at once. }
58. Rh1 { The rook drops back to the corner. Where can your knight jump? } 58... Ng3+ { Check, and the rook in the corner is attacked too. [%cal Gg3e4,Gg3h1] } (58... h4 { You push the pawn, and the fork quietly disappears. } 59. Rg1 Ne7 60. Kf4 { Nothing is left here. This is only a draw. }) (58... Nd6+ { A check as well. But look behind it: there is nothing there. } 59. Kf3 { The king just steps away. A check with one target wins nothing. }) 59. Kf4 { The king has to move, and nothing can guard the corner. } 59... Nxh1 { A whole rook. The fork did all the work. } (59... Ne2+ { You save the knight and forget about the rook. } 60. Ke3 Ng3 61. Ra1 { The rook slipped away. Only a draw. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: fork practice two"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/io8m2o9v"]
[FEN "5q1k/1Q1nN3/4pn1p/3p2p1/1p1PB3/rPb1P1BP/5PP1/5RK1 b - - 0 32"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5q1k/1Q1nN3/4pn1p/3p2p1/1p1PB3/rPb1P1BP/5PP1/5RK1_b_-_-_0_32"]

{ A busy board this time. He is about to take a bishop, and he feels safe. }
32... dxe4 { He takes the bishop. Your knight is looking for a square. } 33. Ng6+ { Check, and the queen sitting behind the king is attacked too. [%cal Gg6h8,Gg6f8] } (33. Nc6 { The knight jumps out, but it does not attack anything that matters. } 33... Qa8 34. Qc7 { The queen walked away and now you are worse. }) (33. Be5 { You attack his knight, but he takes yours on e7 first. } 33... Qxe7 34. Bxf6 Qxf6 { A knight down, and the fork is gone too. }) 33... Kg8 { The king slides across, and the queen still cannot move. } 34. Nxf8 { A queen for a knight. The fork wins again. } (34. Ne5 { The knight steps back and lets the queen live. } 34... Qa8 35. Qc7 { You had a queen in your hands and let it go. }) 1-0


[Event "Basic tactics: the pin, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/qmwSksav"]
[FEN "r1b2rk1/3n1pp1/pq2p2p/1pb5/4B2B/5N2/PPPQ2PP/2KR1R2 w - - 1 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b2rk1/3n1pp1/pq2p2p/1pb5/4B2B/5N2/PPPQ2PP/2KR1R2_w_-_-_1_16"]

{ We will now learn the pin. A pinned piece is stuck: it is not allowed to move away. }
16. Bxa8 { He grabs your rook. Now look at his queen and his king on one diagonal. [%csl Rd2,Rc1][%cal Yc5e3] } 16... Be3 { The bishop pins the queen to the king. She cannot step aside. [%cal Ge3d2,Gd2c1] } (16... Qa7 { You tuck the queen away, but the pin was the whole point. } 17. Qc3 { Nothing has happened, and White is still a rook up. }) (16... a5 { A quiet pawn move while you are a rook down. } 17. Rfe1 b4 18. Kb1 { His king steps off the diagonal and the chance is gone. }) 17. Qxe3 { He must take, because his queen was never allowed to move. [%cal Yb6e3] } 17... Qxe3+ { A queen for a bishop. That is what a pin does. } (17... Qc7 { You forget to take back, and your bishop is simply gone. } 18. Bg3 { A whole piece for nothing. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: pin practice one"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/qFpAnWmi"]
[FEN "2kr2nr/ppp3pp/4N1b1/3p4/8/4P2q/PPP1B3/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 3 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2kr2nr/ppp3pp/4N1b1/3p4/8/4P2q/PPP1B3/R1BQ1RK1_b_-_-_3_18"]

{ This time you are the one a piece down. One bishop move turns the whole game around. }
18... Qxe6 { His queen grabs your knight. Now ask where his king is standing. } 19. Bg4 { The bishop pins the queen: his king is right behind her. [%cal Gg4e6,Ge6c8] } (19. Bd2 { A quiet developing move while you are a piece down. } 19... Nf6 20. Qe1 { His extra knight is starting to count. }) (19. Rf4 { The rook swings across, but it traps nothing at all. } 19... Kb8 20. Bd2 { Nothing gained, and you are still a piece down. }) 19... Qxg4+ { She has to take, and it arrives with check. } 20. Qxg4+ { Take back. A queen for a bishop, and now you are winning. } (20. Kh2 { You step out of the check and forget about the queen. } 20... Qh4+ 21. Kg2 { A queen down, and mate is on the way. }) 1-0


[Event "Basic tactics: the skewer, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/hnHmSl7U"]
[FEN "6k1/5pp1/5bn1/r2B3Q/2pP4/2P3P1/5P2/6K1 w - - 2 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/6k1/5pp1/5bn1/r2B3Q/2pP4/2P3P1/5P2/6K1_w_-_-_2_41"]

{ We will now learn the skewer. Check the king, and take whatever was hiding behind him. }
41. Qh1 { His queen tucks in behind the king. Both are on the bottom row now. [%csl Rg1,Rh1][%cal Ya5a1] } 41... Ra1+ { Check along the bottom row. He must move, and the queen is left bare. [%cal Ga1g1,Ga1h1] } (41... Bxd4 { You grab a pawn with the bishop, but the queen was the real prize. } 42. Qe4 Rxd5 43. Qxd5 { His queen slipped away and took your rook on the way. }) (41... Ra7 { The rook steps back to safety and the moment has gone. } 42. Bxc4 { He takes a pawn, his queen is comfortable, and you are simply worse. }) 42. Kh2 { He steps up. Now take the piece that was standing behind him. [%cal Ya1h1] } 42... Rxh1+ { The queen falls. In a skewer the big piece is in front and has to move. } (42... Ra7 { You retreat and let the queen live. } 43. Qb1 { The skewer has gone and White is fine again. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: skewer practice one"]
[Result "1-0"]
