{ Your rook and his knight face each other down one column. Something is guarding it. }
19. Nh2 { A knight steps back. Now ask what is defending the other one on d2. } 19... Nxe3 { Take the guard. His bishop was the only thing holding d2. [%cal Ge3d2] } (19... Rd7 { You defend your own rook, but his pieces are the better ones. } 20. Nhf1 Rhd8 21. Nb3 { You are worse and the moment has gone. }) (19... b4 { A pawn push that does not touch the knight at all. } 20. Nhf1 { He untangles and you are simply worse. }) 20. Rxe3 { He takes back, and now d2 has nobody looking after it. } 20... Rxd2 { A free knight. The guard had to go first. } (20... Rd5 { You centralise the rook and leave the knight alone. } 21. Ne4 Bg6 22. Nf1 { The knight was there for the taking and you let it go. }) 0-1
